window.docContents[180].resolve({"/Connectivity/#section-the-infinite-path-is-a-tree":{"contents":"Our running example pathNat₁ is a tree. It is connected, since every vertex n can\nbe reached from 0 along the walk pathNat₁.path 0 n. This walk ends at 0 + n,\nwhich is not definitionally n, so we use Walk.copy (Copy) to\nchange its endpoint.\n\nlemma connected_pathNat₁ : pathNat₁.Connected :=\n  (connected_iff_exists_forall_reachable _).2\n    ⟨0, fun n => ⟨(pathNat₁.path 0 n).copy rfl (by simp)⟩⟩\n\n\nTo see that pathNat₁ is acyclic, consider the largest vertex m of a cycle. It has\ntwo neighbours on the cycle (IsCycle.ncard_neighborSet_toSubgraph_eq_two), but its\nonly neighbour that is not larger than m is m - 1.\n\nlemma isAcyclic_pathNat₁ : pathNat₁.IsAcyclic := by\n  intro v c hc\n  -- let m be the largest vertex of the cycle c\n  obtain ⟨m, hm, hmax⟩ := c.support.toFinset.exists_max_image id ⟨v, by simp⟩\n  simp only [List.mem_toFinset, id] at hm hmax\n  -- its cycle neighbours are adjacent to m and at most m, so equal m - 1\n  have hsub : c.toSubgraph.neighborSet m ⊆ {m - 1} := fun w hw => by\n    have := c.toSubgraph.adj_sub hw\n    have := hmax w (c.mem_support_of_adj_toSubgraph hw.symm)\n    grind\n  -- but m has two neighbours on the cycle\n  have := Set.ncard_le_ncard hsub\n  rw [hc.ncard_neighborSet_toSubgraph_eq_two hm, Set.ncard_singleton] at this\n  grind\n\n\nlemma isTree_pathNat₁ : pathNat₁.IsTree := ⟨connected_pathNat₁, isAcyclic_pathNat₁⟩\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity\u0009Trees","header":"10.2.2. The Infinite Path is a Tree","id":"/Connectivity/#section-the-infinite-path-is-a-tree"},"/Subgraphs/#section-which-notion-of-subgraph":{"contents":"We now have four different ways of saying that H is a subgraph of G. Which one\nwe choose can make a big difference to how easy a proof is.\n\n* Representation* Vertex type* Use it when\n* H ≤ G* V* every vertex is kept, e.g. deleting edges\n* G.induce s, H.coe* ↑s, ↑H.verts* you need SimpleGraph theorems\n* H : G.Subgraph* V* the vertex set varies, e.g. matchings\n* Copy A G, A ⊑ G* any type* A is a fixed graph, e.g. A-free graphs\n\n* H ≤ G with H : SimpleGraph V (Order) keeps the vertex type,\nso the whole SimpleGraph API applies and these subgraphs form a Boolean algebra.\nIt is the right choice when all vertices are kept, as for G.deleteEdges s.* G.induce s (Induced Subgraphs via Comap) and H.coe are\ngenuine SimpleGraphs, so every theorem about SimpleGraph applies to them, but\ntheir vertices live in a subtype. We pay for this whenever we pass between v : V\nand ⟨v, hv⟩ : ↑s. The De Bruijn–Erdős theorem below is a typical use: Mathlib\nstates it for the graphs H.coe of finite subgraphs H.* H : G.Subgraph lets the vertex set vary inside V, and the subgraphs form a\nlattice. Use it when Mathlib defines the notion you need for subgraphs, such as\nWalk.toSubgraph or matchings (Subgraph.IsMatching). A matching must be a\nG.Subgraph: its vertex set is the set of matched vertices, and a perfect\nmatching is one that is spanning.* Copy A G is a labelled copy of a graph A whose vertices may have any type, and\nA ⊑ G says that such a copy exists. Use it to talk about containment (or\nnon-containment) of a fixed graph, as in A.Free G.\n\nWe have already met most of the conversions between these: toSubgraph H h turns\nh : H ≤ G into a G.Subgraph, while H.spanningCoe and H.coe turn\nH : G.Subgraph into a SimpleGraph. Copies and subgraphs are related by the\nfollowing three definitions.\n\n#source Copy.toSubgraph -- toSubgraph (f : Copy A B) : B.Subgraph := .map f.toHom ⊤\n#source Copy.isoToSubgraph -- (f : Copy A B) : A ≃g f.toSubgraph.coe\n#source Subgraph.coeCopy -- (H : G.Subgraph) : Copy H.coe G\n\n\nFinally there are useful hybrids. When we define partial colourings in\nPartial Colourings we will use the graph\n((⊤ : G.Subgraph).induce s).spanningCoe whose edges are those of G induced by\ns. Since it is a SimpleGraph V, we can use the SimpleGraph.Coloring API.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs\u0009Subgraphs as Simple Graphs","header":"9.3.4. Which Notion of Subgraph?","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-which-notion-of-subgraph"}});